# Build an Internal App & Task Management System

## 1. Project Objective

Build a modern, premium-looking internal company web application for managing mobile apps, tracking app updates and versions, and organizing team tasks.

This is a combination of:

- **App Management:** Maintain company apps and their release/update history.
- **Task Management:** Manage tasks through customizable Trello-style boards and workflow columns.
- **User Management:** Create and manage team members, profile pictures, and task assignments.

The application must remain simple, intuitive, and easy to use. Do not turn it into an oversized enterprise project management system.

## 2. Technology Stack

Use the following technologies:

- Next.js (latest stable compatible version)
- React
- JavaScript ES6+ and JSX only
- Tailwind CSS
- Next.js App Router
- Supabase PostgreSQL
- Supabase Authentication
- Supabase Storage
- PostgreSQL Row Level Security (RLS)
- Lucide React icons
- dnd-kit for drag-and-drop functionality
- Vercel deployment

Do not use TypeScript, Python, PHP, Django, Flask, or Streamlit.

Do not create a separate Express backend unless absolutely necessary. Use Next.js server-side functionality, API routes, and server actions.

## 3. Application Navigation

Keep the navigation simple:

1. Overview
2. Apps
3. Boards
4. My Tasks
5. Members

Do not add unnecessary navigation items.

Explicitly exclude:
- Settings module
- Notifications module
- Departments
- Teams module
- Reports and analytics modules
- Time tracking
- Project timelines
- Gantt charts

Basic account/profile functionality and necessary authentication controls are allowed, but do not build an unnecessary settings module.

## 4. App Management Module

This is a core module of the application.

The company manages multiple mobile applications. Admins must be able to create, edit, view, and delete app records.

### App information

Each app should support:

- App name
- App icon/logo
- Package name
- Platform (Android, iOS, or both)
- App description
- Google Play Store URL
- Apple App Store URL
- Current version name
- Current version code/build number
- Latest release date
- App status (Active, Inactive, Discontinued)
- Assigned responsible users
- Created date

Allow uploading an app icon using Supabase Storage.

### App details page

Every app must have its own dedicated details page.

Display:

- App icon and name
- Package name
- Platform
- Current version
- Latest release date
- App description
- Assigned team members
- Recent update history

### App version and update tracking

Allow admins and authorized members to record app updates.

Each update record should include:

- Version name (e.g. 2.4.0)
- Version code/build number
- Release date
- Update title
- Update description / changelog
- Update type (New Features, Improvements, Bug Fixes, Maintenance)
- Release status (Planned, In Development, Testing, Released)
- Related tasks, if applicable
- Users responsible for the update

Display updates in reverse chronological order, with the latest release first.

The app's current version should be updated when an authorized user marks a release as Released. Preserve the complete version history.

Users should be able to quickly see:
- Current app version
- Previous versions
- Latest changes
- Upcoming planned updates
- Tasks related to an update

Do not build a complicated release management or deployment pipeline. This is a straightforward internal version and update tracking system.

## 5. User Management Module

Admins must have complete CRUD functionality for users.

### User creation

Admin should be able to create/invite multiple users.

Each user profile should include:

- Full name
- Email address
- Profile picture / DP
- Role (Admin or Member)
- Account status (Active or Inactive)
- Created date

### Profile pictures

Users should be able to upload and update their own profile pictures.

Store profile images in Supabase Storage.

Display profile pictures throughout the application:
- Task cards
- Task details
- Assignee selectors
- App responsible users
- Members page
- Comments

If a user has not uploaded a picture, display their initials as an avatar.

### Admin permissions

Admin must be able to:

- Create/invite users
- View all users
- Edit user details
- Change user roles
- Activate/deactivate users
- Delete or disable accounts safely
- View tasks assigned to each user
- Assign users to apps
- Assign tasks to users

Do not allow members to change their own role or grant themselves administrative privileges.

Use Supabase Auth for authentication. Do not store passwords in the profiles table. Do not enable unrestricted public registration.

## 6. Overview Page

Create a clean landing page that provides a quick overview of the company's work.

Display only useful information:

- Total apps
- Active apps
- Total team members
- Total tasks
- My pending tasks
- Recently updated apps
- Recently created tasks
- Recently completed tasks

Include a section for recently updated applications showing their icon, name, current version, and last update date.

Keep the overview focused. Do not create analytics-heavy dashboards or complicated charts.

## 7. Boards & Task Management

Build a Trello-inspired task management experience.

### Multiple boards

Users should be able to access multiple boards, such as:

- App Development
- Marketing
- QA & Testing
- Product Updates

These are examples only. Do not hardcode them.

Admins and authorized users should be able to create, rename, and delete boards.

Each board can optionally be associated with one or more company apps.

### Dynamic workflow sections

Every board must have its own customizable workflow columns.

Examples:

- Planning
- Development
- QA
- Marketing
- Uploaded at Developer Account
- Completed

These are examples, not fixed departments or predefined modules.

Users with permission must be able to:

- Create columns
- Rename columns
- Reorder columns
- Delete columns
- Move tasks between columns
- Reorder tasks inside columns

The workflow must be completely dynamic.

Do not implement a separate hardcoded task status system. A task's current column represents its workflow stage.

If a column containing tasks is deleted, require the user to move those tasks to another column or confirm an appropriate reassignment before deletion.

### Task cards

Every task should support:

- Task title
- Description
- Assignee or multiple assignees, if enabled
- Priority (Low, Medium, High, Urgent)
- Due date
- Labels
- Associated app (optional)
- Associated app version/update (optional)
- Attachments
- Comments
- Created by
- Created date
- Updated date

Users should be able to:

- Create tasks
- Edit tasks
- Delete tasks according to permissions
- Assign tasks
- Change priorities
- Add descriptions
- Add labels
- Move cards using drag and drop
- Reorder cards
- Open a detailed task view

### Task details

Clicking a card should open a clean modal or side drawer.

Include:
- Task title and description
- Assignees with profile pictures
- Priority
- Due date
- Associated app and version, if any
- Current workflow column
- Comments
- Attachments
- Activity information

Avoid unnecessary complexity.

## 8. App-to-Task Relationship

Apps and tasks must be connected.

A task can optionally belong to a particular app.

For example:

App: PDF Reader

Version: 2.4.0

Related tasks:
- Fix PDF export crash
- Improve image insertion
- Add AI summary feature
- Complete QA testing
- Prepare Play Store release

When viewing an app, users should be able to see its related tasks and updates.

When viewing a task, users should be able to navigate to its associated app.

This relationship should make it easy to understand which tasks are contributing to each app's development and updates.

## 9. Attachments

Allow users to attach:

- Screenshots
- Images
- APK files
- ZIP files
- PDF documents
- Other permitted documents
- External URLs

Store uploaded files in a private Supabase Storage bucket named `pms-attachments`.

Use paths such as:

`boards/{board_id}/cards/{card_id}/{filename}`

Store attachment metadata in PostgreSQL, not Base64 file content.

Use signed URLs for private file access.

Validate file size, file type, permissions, and filenames. APKs must be treated as ordinary downloadable files; never execute or automatically install them.

## 10. Comments

Users should be able to comment on tasks.

Each comment should contain:
- Comment text
- Author
- Author profile picture
- Created timestamp

Allow authorized users to edit or delete their own comments. Admins may moderate comments where appropriate.

## 11. My Tasks

Create a simple page showing tasks assigned to the currently authenticated user.

Display:
- Task title
- Associated app
- Board
- Current workflow column
- Priority
- Due date

Allow users to open tasks directly from this page.

## 12. Permissions & Roles

Keep roles limited to:

**Admin**
- Manage all apps
- Manage app updates
- Manage users
- Create and manage boards
- Manage workflow sections
- Create, assign, edit, and delete tasks
- View all company tasks

**Member**
- View authorized apps and boards
- Create and update permitted tasks
- Move tasks between permitted sections
- Upload attachments
- Comment on tasks
- Update their own profile picture
- View their assigned tasks

Enforce permissions on the server and through PostgreSQL RLS. Hiding UI controls is not sufficient security.

## 13. Supabase Database

Create a complete standalone SQL file:

`supabase/schema.sql`

It must be ready to copy and execute in:

Supabase Dashboard → SQL Editor → New Query → Run.

Include all necessary:

- Tables
- UUID primary keys
- Foreign keys
- Constraints
- Indexes
- TIMESTAMPTZ columns
- Updated-at triggers
- Relationships
- RLS enablement
- Secure RLS policies
- Necessary database functions and secure helper functions

### Suggested database tables

1. `profiles`
2. `apps`
3. `app_members`
4. `app_updates`
5. `boards`
6. `board_members`
7. `board_sections`
8. `cards`
9. `card_labels`
10. `card_comments`
11. `card_attachments`

Add any additional table only when technically necessary.

### Database security requirements

- Link `profiles.id` to `auth.users.id`.
- Do not store passwords.
- Prevent unauthorized public registration.
- Restrict data access to authenticated and authorized users.
- Prevent users from accessing another board's cards by manipulating IDs.
- Prevent unauthorized access to app updates and attachments.
- Prevent privilege escalation.
- Avoid recursive RLS policy problems.
- Ensure Admin permissions are checked securely.
- Use appropriate indexes for frequently queried relationships.

Create an optional `supabase/seed.sql` for sample boards and workflow sections. Never create fake authentication users or hardcode production credentials.

### Supabase Storage

Create/document the following private buckets:

- `pms-attachments` for task files
- `profile-avatars` for user profile pictures
- `app-icons` for application icons

Provide the necessary Storage policies and explain any manual Supabase configuration.

## 14. Environment Variables

Create `.env.example` and use `.env.local` for local development.

Use:

```env
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=YOUR_SUPABASE_PROJECT_URL
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=YOUR_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY

DATABASE_URL=YOUR_SESSION_POOLED_POSTGRES_CONNECTION_STRING

SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=YOUR_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
```

Important implementation requirements:

- `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL` must contain the Supabase Project URL.
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` is the public/publishable client key.
- `DATABASE_URL` must contain the session-pooled PostgreSQL connection string if direct server-side PostgreSQL access is required.
- The session-pooled connection string must never be exposed to browser code.
- `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` must only be used in trusted server-side code and must never be exposed through a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable.
- Prefer Supabase Auth and Supabase client libraries for normal application database operations.
- Do not unnecessarily introduce a second database access method.

Use the environment variables correctly in the Supabase client configuration.

## 15. UI/UX Design

Build a premium, modern SaaS interface inspired by Trello and Linear.

Design principles:

- Clean and uncluttered
- Modern typography
- Consistent spacing
- Professional colors
- Responsive layout
- Smooth interactions
- Clear navigation
- Attractive cards
- Compact tables
- Beautiful profile avatars
- Useful empty states
- Loading skeletons
- Proper success and error feedback
- Confirmation dialogs for destructive actions

The Boards page should have a Kanban-style layout with horizontally scrollable columns.

The Apps page should show application cards with icons, names, versions, and recent update information.

The Members page should display profile pictures, names, email addresses, roles, and account status.

